  "text": "We are given the expression:\n\n$$\n\\frac{72}{3^5 \\cdot 7^2}\n$$\n\nWe are to express this as a **decimal** and find how many **non-zero digits** are to the **right of the decimal point**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Simplify the expression\n\nFirst, compute the denominator:\n\n$$\n3^5 = 243,\\quad 7^2 = 49\n$$\n\nSo the denominator is:\n\n$$\n3^5 \\cdot 7^2 = 243 \\cdot 49\n$$\n\nLet’s compute this:\n\n$$\n243 \\cdot 49 = 243 \\cdot (50 - 1) = 243 \\cdot 50 - 243 = 12150 - 243 = 11907\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n$$\n\\frac{72}{11907}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Convert the fraction to a decimal\n\nWe want to divide:\n\n$$\n\\frac{72}{11907}\n$$\n\nThis is not an easy fraction to divide by hand, so we’ll use a calculator or find a pattern.\n\nWe can do:\n\n$$\n\\frac{72}{11907} \\approx 0.006048...\n$$\n\nLet’s be more precise. Use a calculator or perform the division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{72}{11907} \\approx 0.006048715...\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Identify the non-zero digits to the right of the decimal\n\nThe decimal is approximately:\n\n$$\n0.006048715...\n$$\n\nSo the digits **to the right of the decimal** are:\n\n$$\n006048715...\n$$\n\nWe now look for **non-zero digits** in this sequence:\n\n- 0 → zero\n- 0 → zero\n- 6 → non-zero\n- 0 → zero\n- 4 → non-zero\n- 8 → non-zero\n- 7 → non-zero\n- 1 → non-zero\n- 5 → non-zero\n\nSo the **non-zero digits** are:\n\n$$\n6, 4, 8, 7, 1, 5\n$$\n\nThat’s **6 non-zero digits**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{6}\n$$",
